SM(391,{"n":"Deuteronomy 5 Part 1","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Deuteronomy 5 Part 1.mp3","d":2798.9,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[9.04,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.64,"Okay, we're gonna go ahead and get started."],[24.88,"When we get started, we are picking up in Deuteronomy chapter 5, which as some people have noticed already, is uh going to be the Ten Commandments Redux."],[37.12,"We previously saw the Ten Commandments back in Exodus chapter 20."],[40.8,"And they're gonna be slightly different this time, but we'll talk more about that here when we get started."],[46.32,"In terms of recap, uh, we kind of had a transition."],[51.2,"A recap can be kind of short this time, because we had kind of a transition at the end of uh chapter four. last time, which was a little bit ago now, uh, for those of us in real time"],[65.72,"But Book of Deuteronomy, as we said now several times, is primarily Moses' final address, his final homily, his final sermon to the people of Israel as they're on the plains of Moab preparing to cross the Jordan, come into Canaan under Joshua's leadership."],[86.52,"And so there was a transition last time in that at the beginning of this address for the first few chapters, Moses sort of ran through the history, especially the recent history, of the people of Israel, what they had experienced together since they came out of Egypt in the Exodus until that day."],[110.32,"And we showed how this is obviously recapping things that we've already read about in the preceding books of the Torah, but that Moses was rhetorically highlighting particular things, reminding them of particular things that happened."],[127.92,"He obviously, in those few chapters, did not go through the entirety of what was in Exodus, Leviticus, and Numbers."],[135.1,"But he highlighted certain things, he made a point of certain things, he described those things in certain ways, sometimes in a different way than they had been previously described, in order to make a point about them."],[145.68,"And so that was kind of a quick run-through of their history."],[148.64,"And now we've transitioned"],[151.0,"And really, this begins in earnest here at the beginning of chapter five."],[156.36,"Uh now he's moving on to doing a similar kind of summary and discussion of the commandments of God."],[167.08,"So we saw over the course of particularly the second half of the book of Exodus, uh starting with Exodus 20."],[174.53,"Around the Ten Commandments after Mount Sinai, the beginning of laying out commandments for the people of Israel."],[181.57,"We saw various commandments on various things in Leviticus and even some in Numbers."],[187.03,"Where God gave specific directions on how specific things were going to be handled, and these commandments cover everything from what we would call moral rules, like a lot of the Ten Commandments, but also include how to perform sacrifices, what feasts to celebrate and when to celebrate them and how to celebrate them, what to eat and not to eat, how to deal with mold and mildew, how to do with it, deal with different types of diseases, outbreaks in the camp, all kinds of things."],[217.25,"All kinds of things."],[218.69,"From things that seem very mundane, about day-to-day life."],[223.29,"You use the bathroom, you're unclean until you wash."],[225.94,"Kind of even semi-obvious."],[228.8,"Things, all the way to very detailed moral things about how you treat family members, how you treat your neighbors."],[237.14,"Even things like love for your enemies."],[239.68,"That kind of thing that we've seen, we've seen along the way."],[266.01,"So there is this whole, I guess now we would call it a meme, that started in the Protestant Reformation."],[273.57,"That there are these three different parts of the quote unquote law."],[278.61,"I'm doing a lot of scare quotes here for the recording."],[281.01,"I don't think the mic will pick up my fingers doing this."],[283.89,"Don't think it's that sensitive."],[285.65,"Uh but um that uh there is a uh moral law, a civil law, and a ceremonial law."],[296.22,"And the moral law is, well, this is how people should live their lives, and the civil law is sort of, well, this is how you should run your government and decide court cases, and then the the uh ceremonial law is all the stuff having to do with sacrifices, and we're gonna throw what you're allowed to eat in there too, and and all the molded mildew stuff and skin disease stuff."],[319.9,"We're gonna throw that all in there too."],[321.66,"Not for a super clear reason, but we're gonna throw. that all in there"],[325.38,"And then based on that categorization, they will argue, well, the moral law is sort of eternal and permanent, and Christ fulfills the civil law in one way and the ceremonial law in a different way."],[343.22,"Parts of it at least."],[345.06,"But either way, you don't have to worry about those anymore."],[348.23,"The problem with all that is, as we've seen, like, that's just nowhere in the Bible."],[354.25,"So when our Protestant friends."],[356.25,"Yeah, when our Protestant friends try to talk to us about that, the correct response is: can you show me that in the Bible?"],[361.85,"Because it's just not there."],[363.63,"And you can't even, it's not even grouped nicely, like, okay, we' well, see, here we have three chapters of civil law and here have the right, its all mixed together."],[375.21,"It's all mixed together."],[376.57,"All the things about sexual morality are in Leviticus 18, and then a couple chapters later you've got clean and unclean animals."],[385.46,"Like, there's not they're they're not broken up like this in the text."],[390.82,"And so the same is gonna be true here."],[393.3,"As Moses goes through them, I mean he's gonna be making particular points and there'll be themes, but he's not breaking them down into some kind of categories."],[402.44,"So, like, here's all the rules about how to live your life."],[404.52,"Now I'm gonna talk about feasts and how to celebrate."],[406.86,"Like, this stuff all goes together and is all one piece."],[412.58,"Really. And the other note, and we'll see this as we go, we'll see this right away, really, tonight, when we get into the Ten Commandments and look at the differences between Exodus 20 and Deuteronomy 5 on the Ten Commandments."],[426.66,"There's differences all through this."],[429.06,"And of course, uh, our 19th-century German friends say, well, there's differences because these are from radically different source documents, and whoever compiled them together, although a literary genius just didn't notice all the places where they contradict each other."],[442.82,"Whereas, seems like the first thing we should notice is once again, the last time we read about these commandments being given, it was in the context of the people living for 40 years in the desert, in a camp, in tents, and moving from place to place, nomadically."],[463.28,"Whereas Moses is preparing them to go into the land and settle there."],[469.84,"So things like how you celebrate Posca when you're all living in a big camp in the desert, in tents, and how you celebrate Pasca, Passover, when you're settled down in a country in cities and towns, you know, they're spread out for miles and miles."],[492.49,"That's gonna look a little different."],[495.37,"Just it will, no matter what."],[497.79,"Meaning the same ideas, the same concepts, the same commandments are gonna have to be applied a little differently in those two circumstances."],[506.31,"And so a big part of what Moses is doing here in Deuteronomy, like, why are we rehearsing all this a second time?"],[515.91,"The reason we're getting this a second time is that this second time it's being applied in a different circumstance."],[523.59,"And this is especially helpful to us as modern readers, because what we're always having to do when we read the scriptures, is we're trying to have to figure out the core of what the scripture is teaching and then apply that to our historical circumstances today."],[543.59,"And there's lots of ways to do that wrong"],[549.55,"There are way more ways to do that wrong than there are to do that right."],[554.73,"And so we can make mistakes about what is the core here."],[560.39,"Like we can we can whittle down the core so that it doesn't mean anything anymore."],[565.27,"You could probably think of certain ways that modern people interpret the Bible that are like that, where they sort of dwindle down some commandment to the point that it means something like be nice to people or something."],[576.73,"Or right, because they don't like what it literally says."],[579.61,"So they say, well, it doesn't mean what it literally says."],[581.94,"There's lots of ways to make that mistake, and then there's tons of ways to misapply. it"],[588.12,"Like if you say we all need to move out of our houses and we need to all live in tents, because we need to apply this literally in a big camp in tents and live nomadically."],[596.04,"That would all right."],[598.12,"So both of these are true."],[600.7,"Both of these are true."],[602.3,"And part of the reason why the church fathers are so important is that we have a whole bunch of different church fathers who live in different centuries, in different cities, different cultures."],[614.83,"Who take, what scripture teaches, and they're considered church fathers because they're really good at this."],[624.02,"And they're guided by the Holy Spirit in doing this."],[628.27,"Take these things and apply them to their own circumstances."],[632.35,"So St. John Chrysostom takes this, and he's applying it to people living in Antioch at the end of the 4th century, or Constantinople at the beginning of the 5th century."],[644.69,"We're not living in the same circumstances as late Bronze Age Jewish people."],[652.53,"And then you have Saints and Fathers who are applying this in 15th century Greece, and 19th century Russia and Serbia."],[663.41,"It's the same truths of Scripture."],[666.14,"But they have to be reapplied to different places in context."],[668.94,"This is why I've often said that just like people want to find say, oh, well, Exodus says this and Deuteronomy says this, therefore they disagree."],[679.4,"People want to do that with the church fathers."],[681.26,"Oh, well, this father, this century said that, this father, this."],[683.74,"Well, they're talking to two different groups of people in two different centuries, in two different contexts, addressing two different sets of issues."],[691.88,"And you can apply the same truth to two different situations of context, and the application looks different, but they're applying the same principle."],[701.88,"They don't actually disagree."],[703.54,"And so by watching Moses do this Exodus and Deuteronomy or Leviticus in Deuteronomy or Numbers in Deuteronomy, this gives us an example of how this works."],[717.18,"Just like when we read The Church Fathers Applying Scripture, the idea is not that, oh, we just pair it."],[723.42,"No, the way they did this in 19th century Russia is the way we need to do it today."],[727.18,"Or the way they did this in 4th century Anarch is the way we did it today."],[730.38,"No, we're supposed to watch what they did and how they did it, and now take those same truths that apply them to 21st century United States or Canada or Australia or wherever else you may be listening to this recording later."],[746.31,"That's right. That's the goal."],[749.51,"And so Moses is kind of uh putting on a master class, sub rosa here."],[757.37,"This is the earliest. example we have of the interpretation and application of scripture if you think about it"],[765.73,"Because it's a interpreting and applying the other four books of the Torah, which are the first scripture."],[772.05,"And so that's what Deuteronomy is in a certain way."],[775.49,"Okay. So as we pick up here tonight, we'll be picking up with the Ten Commandments."],[780.21,"We'll talk more about the Ten Commandments here when we officially start."],[784.05,"So unless anybody has any leftover comments, questions, book adaptation to movie reviews, we will uh pick up at the beginning of Deuteronomy chapter 5, verse 1."],[800.85,"Now Moses called all Israel and said to them, Hear Is,rael O, the ordinances and the judgments I speak in your hearing on this day, and you shall learn them and be careful to do them."],[812.01,"The Lord our God made a covenant with you in Horeb."],[815.53,"The Lord did not make this covenant with your fathers, but with you, all of you alive here today."],[821.69,"The Lord spoke to you face to face on the mountain from the midst of the fire."],[825.85,"I stood between the Lord and you at that time to declare to you the words of the Lord, for you were afraid because of the fire, and you did not go up the mountain."],[835.93,"And he said, we'll pause there."],[840.09,"So we've already got a couple of important things here in how Moses has framed this."],[844.75,"So he says, Here I'm gonna announce the ordinances, the commandments of God to you today."],[847.95,"But then he says, these are the ones you already heard before"],[853.89,"And notice he says, you, not your fathers."],[856.85,"Yeah. Because as we've commented before, technically speaking, that's not true."],[863.75,"Because this covenant at Mount Horeb, was 40 years ago."],[870.3,"And that 40 years was deliberately so that that generation would die off."],[876.3,"These are their children."],[878.7,"But notice the theme here."],[881.06,"The covenant is between God and them, not between God and their fathers, their ancestors."],[889.71,"And so this is an enduring thing"],[895.25,"And we've talked about this before in terms of how the feasts and everything are structured."],[899.73,"At Passover, it's not, this is the night that our ancestors were brought out of Egypt."],[905.65,"This is the night when God brought us, like us at the table out of Egypt."],[912.51,"Because the idea is that we are participants in these events as we celebrate them."],[920.51,"Now, in this case, what was the celebration?"],[922.43,"This wasn't Passover."],[924.11,"Pentecost is when the giving of the Torah was celebrated."],[932.12,"And the covenant."],[934.64,"And so, yes, so technically speaking, historically speaking, God did not speak to them, the people hearing Moses that day, face to face on the mountain."],[947.92,"Moses did not stand between those people and God on that day."],[952.45,"But he did because they've all become participants, in that covenant."],[960.23,"Now we've talked about the word covenant before."],[962.15,"It's especially important."],[963.27,"Here's that structure."],[966.04,"In terms of the Ten Commandments that we're about to read and the book of Deuteronomy as a whole."],[971.4,"Okay So the word covenant is used and has been used pretty much back to the beginning of English translations of the Bible to translate the Hebrew word berit, which is usually transliterated B-E-R-I-T-H."],[994.88,"The uh corresponding Greek word is deathiki."],[999.52,"Deathiki is also the word that gets translated Testament as an Old Testament, New Testament."],[1005.52,"So you will see some translations, for example, the Divine Liturgy will say, This is my blood of the New Testament."],[1012.4,"But when people hear New Testament in English, they think of part of the Bible."],[1018.08,"They don't think of a covenant."],[1019.94,"Per se. Which is why using covenant there is a better translation."],[1024.26,"If you don't agree with me, you're wrong."],[1028.26,"So I'm in good stead."],[1032.42,"But even what that is a little unclear."],[1037.78,"So the original context, Bari, is not just referring to sometimes people will say a covenant."],[1043.06,"Well, that's a contract or an agreement, or and it makes us think of sort of modern business dealings, and that's not really where the word comes from in the Bronze Age."],[1054.34,"Where it comes from is a particular type of treaty document."],[1059.94,"I've talked about this in the Bible study, I know before, but it's especially pertinent here."],[1064.42,"It's impertinent in terms of the whole structure of Deuteronomy, and I said I was going to talk more about this in the intro to Deuteronomy, but it's doubly pertinent here with the Ten Commandments."],[1073.58,"It's a particular type of treaty that we have examples of, a series of examples of among the Hittites from the mid 2nd millennium BC, so circa very roughly 1500 BC."],[1088.46,"And one of the signatories of these treaties is named Shupa Liliuma."],[1094.86,"So he was a pagan, so you can't name your kid that baptismally, unfortunately."],[1099.42,"Yeah You can maybe call your kid Shoopy Doop or something."],[1102.3,"I don't know."],[1103.26,"But these are treaties that were issued by a great king or a conquering king, two vassal kings."],[1114.82,"Okay. So we've talked about the the early empires that we're gonna see throughout the Old Testament."],[1124.67,"Or the Babylonian Empire that was around in the second millennium BC."],[1129.95,"Uh weren't empires like the Roman Empire."],[1133.07,"So we think about maybe the Roman Empire, where there's sort of one guy who's on the top."],[1139.49,"There's this emperor, and then everybody else is sort of delegated lackeys."],[1145.55,"Down down from him."],[1147.61,"And that's not how it worked in the ancient world."],[1150.09,"For a lot of reasons, the biggest one was infrastructure."],[1153.47,"Like the infrastructure of that is very difficult and very difficult to maintain."],[1159.11,"So what was far more common was you would have a king of a prominent city, a large city, a prosperous city, a militarily powerful city, who would, through his military power, through his trade power, through the natural resources he had available to him, whatever, be able to extend his influence over neighboring towns and cities and villages."],[1187.31,"And he would extend that influence essentially in something far more like the mob today than what we usually think of as statecraft, in the sense that he would exact essentially tribute, taxes, however you want to look at it."],[1208.12,"He would extort yeah, insurance money, yeah, from those other cities and villages to maintain a good relationship and make sure that the larger or prominent city did not have to use its various types of power to you know crush this other entity."],[1228.28,"And so if they could, they would leave whoever was the king of that city, whoever the leader was."],[1235.4,"If they could make a deal, they would leave leave him in power."],[1239.32,"If that person was recalcitrant, then he would be done away with and replaced with someone who would do business."],[1246.05,"And so that's how these early we call them empires, but that's really how they worked."],[1253.09,"And that's why, we'll see, we already saw this a little bit."],[1255.89,"We saw this back all the way back in Genesis with the uh the War of the Five Kings."],[1261.57,"Remember when the kings of the north came and attacked Sodom and and uh Gomorrah and the other cities of the plain, and Lot got taken captive, and Abraham had to go rescue him."],[1271.49,"What happened there at the beginning of the story, is that those five northern cities, powerful, wealthy, and had sort of done this, had sort of subjugated in this way the uh the cities of the plain, including Sodom and Gomorrah."],[1288.41,"And uh the cities of the plain had sort of smelled weakness, maybe, in the people extorting money from them and had decided not to pay."],[1300.65,"And so those northern kings had responded to not receiving their payments by coming down and taking what they wanted, including the kings of the South and other people as slaves and stealing their goods, and extracting that by force."],[1318.23,"So that's what happened."],[1318.91,"So that's that's the kind of thing that we're talking about."],[1321.13,"The Rightesioanus speech states."],[1322.73,"And so the higher king, the king who's projecting his influence, however, he's doing it, is referred to in terms of this structure as the suzerain, and then the lesser kings, the rulers of the lesser cities from whom he's extracting."],[1342.34,"Um they are referred to as vassals."],[1346.34,"Okay, so this is a suzerain vassal treaty or a suzerainty tree."],[1351.62,"Um and so this was not really an agreement in the sense that both sides negotiated and came to an agreement."],[1360.58,"This is something that was given by the suzera, given by the power party in power."],[1365.9,"To it was the terms dictated to the lower entity."],[1372.51,"And it had a very particular structure."],[1377.03,"It started with an introduction that said basically, the suzerain, the higher king, said, Here's who I am."],[1386.36,"And here are the good things that I have done for you."],[1390.28,"These, in terms of the human treaties, were often from a certain perspective."],[1394.58,"I have brought peace to your lands."],[1396.42,"I've made sure nothing happens to you or your family or your flocks."],[1401.3,"Isn't that nice of me?"],[1403.59,"But laying out these things."],[1404.91,"H'eres what I here's who I am."],[1406.03,"H'eres what I've done for you."],[1407.64,"Then it would dictate the responsibilities of the lower party."],[1415.08,"And gratitude to me for what I've just said, you are going to do da da da."],[1421.14,"And then it would outline here are the good and wonderful things that will happen if you do all these things I've told you to do."],[1428.82,"Here are the really bad things that will happen if you don't do the things that I've told you to do."],[1435.3,"And we talked about this a little bit in back in Exodus 20."],[1443.92,"Of the law."],[1444.72,"And how that isn't like, oh, God put four of them on the one tablet and then ran out of room."],[1450.96,"And so the other six commandments are on the other."],[1453.46,"That's it's two copies."],[1455.6,"Originally, of the two copies, one was was put at the feet of the idol of the god who called to bear witness to it."],[1467.4,"The other one was given to the king who was receiving it."],[1471.16,"So he would have the terms in front of him."],[1472.92,"The vassal king."],[1473.88,"So the vassal king would have his responsibilities in front of him."],[1477.48,"There would be a divine figure, who was called to be a judge to enforce right the agreement."],[1484.77,"Okay, so the two copies, in this case, both copies are put into the Ark of the Covenant because the Ark of the Covenant is the footstool of God's throne."],[1494.53,"And God is both the king and the god."],[1499.25,"R rightight,? Who is going to enforce it?"],[1501.09,"Okay. But so the book of Deuteronomy. as a whole, this is what I said I was going to talk more about in the introduction"],[1508.33,"The book of Deuteronomy as a whole follows this format."],[1516.49,"Chapters 28 through 30 are blessings and curses."],[1519.45,"It's literally, you do all these things that have been laid out here."],[1524.87,"Here's the good things that will happen, here are the bad things that will happen."],[1527.35,"Well, I'm working in reverse."],[1528.31,"I'll go forward."],[1529.83,"We just finished in the first four chapters, Moses recounting what?"],[1534.79,"God bringing them out of Egypt, bringing them through the wilderness, bringing them to this land, who God is and all the things He's done for them."],[1541.91,"Now we're starting the commandments."],[1543.83,"s. Here' all the things Israel is responsible to do"],[1546.97,"When we get to chapter 28."],[1548.83,"Here are the blessings that will come."],[1551.31,"If you walk in this way and do these things, and then the curses, here are the bad things that will happen."],[1558.67,"And in the case of Deuteronomy, it's when you don't, because God saw it coming."],[1563.95,"When you don't, here's all the things that will happen."],[1566.21,"So the book of Deuteronomy as a whole follows this."],[1569.97,"But the Ten Commandments that we're about to read, that's why I'm doing this here, so you can have it in the back of your head, as a microcosm, follow the same format."],[1581.09,"Because spoilers, the next verse I'm going to read: I am the Lord your God who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of slavery, identifying who he is and what he did for them."],[1589.57,"Then you have a list of commandments."],[1591.94,"Then you have what.'s going to happen"],[1594.62,"So the Ten Commandments have been treated as the reason the Ten Commandments have been treated as a summary of the Torah or a summary of the covenant is because that's how they're presented."],[1610.94,"Especially here in Deuteronomy."],[1623.9,"So it's presented in format as a sort of summary."],[1629.7,"And this has always been seen and been true."],[1634.36,"When the person comes to Christ and says, What good thing must I do to inherit eternal life?"],[1642.96,"And Christ says to him, Well, what is in the Torah?"],[1645.6,"What do you read?"],[1646.96,"He says, Do not commit adultery, do not steal, do not."],[1650.54,"Ten commandments. He's a bunch of the Ten Commandments."],[1655.38,"He says, All these things I have kept from my youth."],[1657.39,"So. They understand, you know, quick summary, okay."],[1661.43,"Here we go."],[1662.39,"And you find this in the church fathers, you find this all through church history."],[1666.57,"Even down to go to our bookstore, buy one of the little red prayer books."],[1671.69,"If you go to the preparation for going to confession, what does it have?"],[1676.89,"The Ten Commandments."],[1678.11,"And then after each of the commandments, it says, Have you done this?"],[1680.95,"Have you done that?"],[1681.67,"to help you sort of think through."],[1683.76,"Different areas of your life."],[1685.22,"But it's using the Ten Commandments as a sort of summary."],[1688.36,"Of subheadings. So that's not something that just happens."],[1691.48,"That's something that's built into the place they are in the text."],[1697.64,"So that's why I ran through that again here, sort of as we're about to get started."],[1704.2,"This is not an agreement between a king and peasants."],[1712.28,"Yeah, the the the royal priesthood part, the kingdom of priests part."],[1716.3,"So we've talked about the priest part before, in the sense that. they were called to"],[1723.06,"What they were called to was that the people of Israel would relate to the non-Israelites, the people of the rest of the nations, the way the priests related to the regular Israelites."],[1735.08,"And so, for example, the Feast of Tabernacles, they'd offer the 70 bowls to God for the 70 nations."],[1741.88,"So the idea was not, sorry, Calvinists, God loved Israel and hated everybody else."],[1750.6,"God was saving Israel and everybody else is going to hell."],[1753.4,"God was saving the world through Israel."],[1759.57,"God was saving the world through Israel"],[1763.09,"And, Christ says that."],[1764.03,"says that He to St."],[1764.83,"Photidi, the Samaritan woman at the well"],[1769.57,"When she asks him about the temple on Mount Gerizim versus the temple in Jerusalem."],[1774.85,"He says, you worship what you do not know, we worship him who we know, because salvation is from the Jews."],[1781.67,"That that's historically."],[1783.99,"But it was always aimed at, as we've seen over and over again, always aimed at the whole world."],[1789.45,"God was revealing himself to the Egyptians."],[1792.78,"The other nations were seeing, we're watching God free the people of Israel from Egypt."],[1800.51,"God is always aimed at the whole world."],[1802.77,"It's Israel through whom he's, through whom he's doing."],[1807.25,"Okay, that priesthood part"],[1808.97,"This, by the way, is the whole missing piece in all of these arguments about extra ecclesium nullisalis."],[1818.87,"Which is the famous quote from St."],[1820.07,"Cyprian outside the church there is no salvation that's causing all this ecclesial anxiety among our Protestant friends currently, apparently."],[1828.07,"If YouTube is to be believed."],[1831.43,"Which it may not be, but with that, you know, that people who are church, we say the Orthodox Church is Christ's church."],[1839.81,"It is the church."],[1840.77,"We say outside of the church, meaning the Orthodox Church, there is no salvation."],[1845.1,"And people hear that as we're saying everyone who's not a member of the Orthodox Church goes to hell."],[1852.26,"Okay. But this just shows a complete lack of biblical understanding."],[1859.56,"Our Protestant friends really need to get to know the Bible better."],[1862.86,"Because you could say at the point in history we're talking about, or anywhere in the Old Testament, right up to when Christ is talking to St."],[1871.02,"Fotini, that there's no salvation outside of Israel."],[1877.1,"But yet, lo and behold, you look all through the pages of the Old Testament, there are people who find salvation."],[1885.8,"Who are not Israelites."],[1887.8,"Because that's not what that means."],[1891.16,"We're not talking about, right right? what individual people face what individual fate in God's judgment"],[1900.88,"We're talking about where is the place where God, the place in the world where God is working out salvation."],[1910.16,"Okay. The New Testament, that's still Israel, but now we call Israel the church."],[1917.3,"That's the place where God's working it out."],[1921.07,"And so the idea that there's somebody who finds salvation, who's not in a member of the organizational, maybe has never heard of the Orthodox Church, does nothing, has nothing to do with the fact that there's no salvation outside. the Orthodox Church"],[1943.11,"Because the Orthodox Church is the place where Christ is and where he's working salvation."],[1949.08,"I.e. anyone who finds salvation finds salvation through Christ, not through someone else or something else."],[1958.02,"And through what Christ is doing in the Orthodox Church, even if they're not in the Orthodox Church."],[1963.38,"That's what we're saying."],[1966.18,"And we're saying somebody asked."],[1969.0,"If someone in the 9th century BC said, I have heard of this God, Yahweh who created the universe."],[1977.8,"I want to know more about him."],[1979.0,"I want to follow him and follow his ways."],[1981.0,"Where would you tell them to go?"],[1981.56,"Probably Jerusalem in particular."],[1987.67,"Like that's where you want to go."],[1990.53,"And you want to study the scriptures there."],[1993.47,"That God gave through Moses."],[1995.39,"And you want to, go and participate as you can."],[1999.11,"In the worship and in the right, and come to know a better way of life."],[2003.48,"And but you probably want to become an Israelite."],[2006.3,"Move there permanently, become an Israelite, become a part."],[2009.31,"That's what you would say."],[2012.08,"You wouldn't say, Oh, no, don't worry about it."],[2014.48,"Just keep hanging out in Moab, and you know, I know they're worshiping a moon god and stuff, and you know, like, but just don't worry about it."],[2021.84,"It's all good"],[2022.04,"You're good. God, you can't limit God."],[2024.5,"Come on. You know, He's the God of the whole world."],[2027.14,"It's all good."],[2028.79,"Just be a good be a good person, you know."],[2030.89,"You wouldn't say that."],[2032.11,"So same thing's true now."],[2034.99,"Don't know somebody that says, I love Christ."],[2037.15,"I want to come to know him more deeply."],[2038.83,"I want to salvation."],[2041.55,"I'm struggling with these sins."],[2042.51,"I want to have a crother."],[2043.39,"What do I do?"],[2044.51,"Come to the Orthodox Church."],[2045.95,"That's what I tell you."],[2047.65,"Because that's where Christ's doing it."],[2049.41,"I don't tell you, no, you're fine where you are."],[2051.49,"You got you gotta have Jesus over there, wherever over there is."],[2056.53,"But of course we don't say that."],[2058.98,"Um, but so yeah, that's the priesthood part."],[2062.18,"But the royal part we don't talk about as much."],[2064.02,"And again, this is what they're called to be"],[2069.6,"Called to be."],[2070.88,"Not that they achieve it per se."],[2073.42,"But they're called to also exercise this ruling function, which is what?"],[2079.58,"Which is putting things in order, which is enacting justice in the world, in relation to the other, the other nations."],[2088.64,"Remember, they're supposed to look at Israel's way of life, and the way Israel conducts itself and say, I gotta find out what that's all about."],[2102.05,"In that that ruling function."],[2104.94,"They're to live like. no other people on the face of the earth were living at the time"],[2108.66,"Treat people the way no other people treat other people."],[2111.84,"Justice, decency, goodness."],[2114.3,"All these things that are characterized."],[2115.9,"This also carries through the church, by the way."],[2120.92,"John says they'll know we're Christians by our love, not by our bumper stickers and t shirts, not by our voting habits."],[2128.9,"Not by our right."],[2130.9,"People should look at Christians and say, look at the way we live and be envious of that and desirous of that for themselves."],[2138.34,"And insofar as that's not the case, insofar as we're just like them, that means we're not right living up to our calling."],[2147.3,"But yeah, that covenant structure is part of that."],[2148.74,"part of It's that, here's who you're called to be."],[2155.27,"Um, and as St."],[2158.23,"Peter says, part of what we're doing as we work out our salvation is trying to make our calling an election sure."],[2165.19,"God has chosen us to do something, he's called us to do something."],[2169.91,"That doesn't mean he does it for us or to us."],[2172.97,"That means we're called to do it, and we're chosen to do it."],[2176.67,"So whether you're a teacher in a classroom with students, or you're right, which we've got a couple of here."],[2182.53,"Or you're talking about a husband and father in a family, or you're talking about right, running a business, you're talking about having some office of authority in the church, you're talking about how right, any of that, yeah, our exercise of that authority is to be something that people look at and go, that's not, you know, the Lords of the Gentiles."],[2202.62,"Lord it over them, so as not to be true of you."],[2206.02,"Um, Christ says, yeah."],[2209.06,"One more note on the Ten Commandments, and then we'll get into them."],[2214.18,"And that's, I know we talked about this a little at Exodus 20, so this is kind of review, but it's a review from a long time ago from the perspective of the people sitting here."],[2222.1,"That's like probably a year."],[2224.34,"What are the issues with the Ten Commandments?"],[2227.7,"No, there's two issues really."],[2229.22,"Number one, they're originally not called the Ten Commandments, they're called the Ten Words."],[2238.48,"And the reason that's important is because of the bigger issue, which there's not really ten of them."],[2245.52,"There's only kind of nine."],[2247.7,"There really are only kind of nine."],[2249.94,"And so because of that, different religious traditions split them up different ways to get ten."],[2257.54,"Because even though when you look at the text, both here and in Exodus 20, you really only have nine things, nine commandments."],[2265.99,"They've always been referred to as the ten words."],[2269.35,"So they get constantly referred to as the ten words, but then you go out and you look them up and you only see nine things."],[2274.15,"Okay. And so then led your domain ensues."],[2278.64,"So uh for uh Jewish folks, that first statement, I am the Lord your God who brought you out of the land of Egypt, the one we're about to read, that's the first commandment."],[2293.6,"Or the first word, even though there's no commandment there."],[2297.28,"And they say, well, technically it does it isn't ten commandments, it's ten words, and so that's a word."],[2301.92,"Yeah. And so that's the way around that."],[2305.57,"And then so then the other the nine that are actually there are then the other nine."],[2309.81,"Um if you are a uh a lot of Protestants, or interestingly enough, Orthodox like us, then uh the part about you shall have no other gods before me, you shall not make for yourself a graven image."],[2335.87,"Those are two different we split those two to get ten, even though grammatically that's they're one."],[2344.75,"We split those into two things."],[2347.01,"No other gods and no idolatry as two separate commandments."],[2351.41,"Okay, if you are Roman Catholic or Lutheran, you split the last one."],[2360.97,"Because we'll use this version, you have to turn a page in most of the Orthodox. Study Bibles if that's what you're looking at"],[2369.05,"But in chapter 5, verse 21, you shall not covet your neighbor's wife."],[2374.25,"And you shall not covet your neighbor's house, his field, his da-da-da-da-da."],[2377.75,"You notice how wife is separate there?"],[2380.15,"There's a thou shalt not, and then a thou shalt not."],[2382.71,"That's where they split it."],[2384.87,"Okay. Now the problem with splitting that one."],[2389.05,"Grammatically that works because it says thou shalt not twice."],[2392.81,"But there's two problems really."],[2394.33,"Number one, it's all about coveting."],[2399.97,"Okay. It's all about coveting."],[2402.13,"You can say, like, well, coveting your neighbor's wife is I guess a little different than coveting those other things."],[2409.81,"Because there would be some kind of yeah, you already kind of covered that with adultery"],[2416.49,"But you can see how it's a little different than coveting his house."],[2420.71,"Uh the problem is the version in Exodus has house first."],[2424.63,"So you get if you split it there, even though it chromatically works, you've got two different ninth commandments depending on which version you're doing."],[2432.87,"So the correct way to do it is, of course, the orthodox way of doing it."],[2436.23,"But honestly, there's only kind of nine."],[2441.16,"And you know, we can flesh out ideas from from there in different ways."],[2447.0,"But so now, let's get into it."],[2449.16,"All of that preamble done."],[2452.28,"I am the Lord. your God who brings you from the land of Egypt out of the house of bondage"],[2458.48,"So that's identifying who he is, what he's done."],[2463.28,"Then there shall be no."],[2466.08,"Here we go."],[2468.0,"Fun with the Orthodox Study Bible translation."],[2470.48,"There shall be no different gods before my face."],[2474.72,"Which is not really a coherent sentence."],[2477.52,"Like all the gods before his face have to be the same."],[2484.88,"So the face thing is doing a really literal yes translation of what usually gets translated as before me."],[2497.04,"The the Hebrew phrase that normally gets translated as the presence of God."],[2503.02,"As we've been reading along and all through the Old Testament, literally says the face of God is what it literally says in Hebrew."],[2509.9,"So when it says, you know, Jonah got up and fled from the Lord's presence, it literally says he got up and fled from Yahweh's face."],[2520.38,"But so that's right, an expression."],[2524.48,"That means his presence before him, that he could see, God can see everything everywhere."],[2530.6,"Like, and so the idea here, and then different instead of other or alien, which is the actual idea."],[2540.58,"Meaning.ing you don't br in any other, worship any other gods alongside him"],[2547.66,"Okay. That's the core of this."],[2550.94,"And that has two pieces, but the core of it is the idea that they're not to worship any other gods, they're only to worship Yahweh the God of Israel."],[2561.74,"This says nothing about whether or not those gods exist in some way or some form, but they're only to worship Yahweh, the God of Israel, which is a break from, of course, the pagan understanding."],[2574.38,"It was not uncommon for a group of people, a given people, group, or tribe, to only have one or two gods."],[2583.1,"That's usually it."],[2584.88,"Cities, major. cities had one or two gods"],[2588.58,"So we think of this Justice League version of the Pantheon where it's like all these guys living on a mountain with superpowers, different abilities."],[2597.86,"That's never how any ancient pagan religion worked."],[2602.74,"Any given place."],[2604.52,"Any given city, any given people group had one or two gods."],[2609.0,"Um, as you get larger, and there were then lesser spirits associated with springs and brooks and glades and trees and different mountains."],[2618.44,"And as you get larger political units, of cities in league with each other, or cities dominated by a bigger city, then what they start doing is they take the gods of those individual cities and say they're related to each other in different ways that parallel the relationships between those cities."],[2638.11,"So if you have this higher king and then you've got vassals underneath him, well, the god of the city that's on top is the father of the gods of the cities who are below."],[2651.31,"They're all siblings."],[2654.0,"So you end up with like Zeus on top, and then Athena, Apollo."],[2660.78,"The gods of all these other cities sort of beneath, and they're said to be siblings of each other."],[2666.4,"But it's not that they worshipped a group."],[2669.85,"It's that they saw the gods of these cities and people groups as being related to each other."],[2674.61,"And so a pagan who was born in one place would worship the god or a couple of gods of that place."],[2683.78,"But then if his family was from there but he moved somewhere else, he would still honor and worship those gods of where they came from, but also those of wherever he moved to."],[2697.24,"And if he stopped in a city while he was on a trip, he would stop off at the local temple and worship those gods."],[2707.14,"And so, in the normal course of events, as we've seen, the people who are making up this new nation of Israel are not all ethnically, in our modern sense."],[2715.32,"United Some of these are descended from Abraham and worship Abraham's God, who, by the way, as we've seen, is the same God who, like the Midianites, like Jethro were worshiping."],[2731.91,"The Edomites, these other groups descended from Abraham, were worshiping the god of Abraham, Yahweh."],[2736.79,"So we're worshiping this God."],[2738.47,"But there's also Egyptians in here."],[2741.37,"And other people who they picked up on the way, who maybe in the past have worshipped, you know."],[2746.61,"Well, I'm from Egypt, but now I'm an Israelite."],[2749.37,"So, you know, I'm gonna take some of those Egyptian gods my family used to worship back in Egypt, whatever city I'm from, I'm gonna worship those, and I'm gonna worship this new god Yahweh now that I'm an Israelite."],[2758.97,"No No. That's the idea here."],[2763.97,"We're not importing anything, we're not bringing anything along with us."],[2767.01,"Clean break, you're a new people."],[2769.65,"Yahweh, God of Israel is the only God who you're worshiping."],[2773.03,"That is it."],[2776.39,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2783.75,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2789.11,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.64,"lv":2,"h":"Deut 5 - Ten Commandments redux","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up <b>Deuteronomy 5</b> = the <b>Ten Commandments Redux</b></li><li class=\"d1\">first seen in <b>Exodus 20</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"slightly different this time\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Recap short - <b>transition at end of ch. 4</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Deuteronomy = Moses' <b>final address / homily / sermon</b></li><li class=\"d1\">plains of <b>Moab</b>, about to cross <b>Jordan</b> into Canaan under <b>Joshua</b></li></ul>"},{"t":65.72,"lv":3,"h":"Recap: history, now commandments","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">First chapters: Moses ran through <b>recent history</b> since the Exodus</li><li class=\"d1\">recapping Exodus-Numbers, but <b>rhetorically highlighting</b> things</li><li class=\"d1\">did not go through everything - <b>made points</b>, described things differently at times</li><li class=\"d0\">Now <b>chapter 5 in earnest</b>: summary and discussion of the <b>commandments of God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Commandments from Exodus 20 on, Leviticus, Numbers cover everything</li><li class=\"d1\">moral rules, <b>sacrifices</b>, feasts, what to eat, <b>mold and mildew</b>, diseases in camp</li><li class=\"d1\">mundane: \"use the bathroom, you're unclean until you wash\"</li><li class=\"d1\">detailed moral: family, neighbors, even <b>love for enemies</b></li></ul>"},{"t":266.01,"lv":2,"h":"The three-part \"law\" meme","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">A \"meme\" from the <b>Protestant Reformation</b> - three parts of the \"law\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>moral</b>, <b>civil</b>, <b>ceremonial</b></li><li class=\"d1\">moral = how to live; civil = run government, court cases; ceremonial = sacrifices - \"throw\" food laws, mold, skin disease in too</li><li class=\"d0\">Then: moral is <b>eternal</b>; Christ fulfills civil one way, ceremonial another → \"don't have to worry\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: <b>nowhere in the Bible</b></li><li class=\"d1\">response: \"can you show me that in the Bible?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not even grouped: <b>sexual morality in Leviticus 18</b>, clean/unclean animals a couple chapters later</li><li class=\"d0\">Same in Deuteronomy: <b>themes</b> but no categories</li><li class=\"d1\">rules for life, then feasts - \"all one piece\"</li></ul>"},{"t":412.58,"lv":2,"h":"Why Exodus and Deuteronomy differ","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Differences \"all through this\" - esp. <b>Exodus 20 vs Deuteronomy 5</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>19th-century German</b> view: radically different <b>source documents</b></li><li class=\"d1\">compiler \"a literary genius\" who didn't notice the contradictions</li><li class=\"d0\">First thing to notice instead: <b>different circumstances</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Exodus: <b>40 years in desert</b>, camp, tents, nomadic</li><li class=\"d1\">Deuteronomy: preparing to <b>settle in the land</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Example: <b>Passover</b> in a camp vs in <b>cities and towns</b> spread over miles</li><li class=\"d1\">same ideas, commandments → <b>applied differently</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why a second time? <b>Applied in a different circumstance</b></li></ul>"},{"t":523.59,"lv":3,"h":"Applying scripture today","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Helpful for us: always <b>finding the core</b> and applying it to our circumstances</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Way more ways to do it wrong</b> than right</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>whittle down</b> the core till it means \"be nice to people\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>misapply</b>: \"move out of our houses and live in tents\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Both are errors</li></ul>"},{"t":602.3,"lv":2,"h":"Church fathers as examples","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Church fathers</b> - many centuries, cities, cultures</li><li class=\"d1\">good at taking scripture and applying it to their circumstances, <b>guided by the Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Chrysostom</b>: Antioch (end 4th c.), Constantinople (early 5th c.)</li><li class=\"d1\">saints in <b>15th c. Greece</b>, <b>19th c. Russia and Serbia</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same truths, <b>reapplied</b> to place and context</li><li class=\"d0\">Don't say \"Exodus says this, Deuteronomy says that - disagree\"</li><li class=\"d1\">same with fathers: different groups, centuries, issues → <b>same principle</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Goal: not copy \"the way they did it in 19th c. Russia\" - <b>watch how</b> → apply to <b>21st c. US, Canada, Australia</b></li></ul>"},{"t":749.51,"lv":3,"h":"Moses' \"master class\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Moses = \"master class, sub rosa\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Earliest example of interpreting and applying scripture</b></li><li class=\"d1\">applying the <b>other four books of the Torah</b>, the first scripture</li><li class=\"d0\">That is what Deuteronomy is</li></ul>"},{"t":800.85,"lv":2,"h":"Deut 5:1-5 - Covenant made with you","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reads <b>5:1-5</b>: \"Hear, O Israel…\" ordinances and judgments</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>covenant at Horeb</b> - \"not with your fathers, but with you, all of you alive here today\"</li><li class=\"d1\">spoke <b>face to face</b> from the fire; Moses stood <b>between</b> - they were afraid</li><li class=\"d0\">Moses' framing: \"announce\" commandments - but ones you <b>already heard</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"You, not your fathers\" - technically <b>not true</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Horeb was <b>40 years ago</b>; that generation <b>died off</b> deliberately</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>their children</b> here</li><li class=\"d0\">Theme: covenant is <b>enduring</b>, between God and <b>them</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Passover</b>: \"this is the night God brought <b>us</b> out\" - <b>participants</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Torah/covenant celebrated at <b>Pentecost</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Historically not face to face - but they've <b>become participants</b></li></ul>"},{"t":960.23,"lv":3,"h":"Covenant = berit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">English <b>covenant</b> translates Hebrew <i>berit</i></li><li class=\"d1\">Greek <i>diatheke</i> - also translated <b>Testament</b> (Old/New)</li><li class=\"d1\">Divine Liturgy: \"my blood of the <b>New Testament</b>\" - people hear a book; <b>covenant</b> is better</li><li class=\"d0\">Not a modern <b>contract or agreement</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Bronze Age origin: a <b>treaty document</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1054.34,"lv":2,"h":"Suzerain-vassal treaties","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hittite treaties, mid <b>2nd millennium BC</b> (~<b>1500 BC</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">signatory <b>Suppiluliuma</b> - pagan, \"can't name your kid that baptismally\"</li><li class=\"d1\">issued by a <b>great king / conquering king</b> to <b>vassal kings</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Relevant to <b>structure of all Deuteronomy</b> and the <b>Ten Commandments</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1114.82,"lv":2,"h":"How early \"empires\" worked","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not like the <b>Roman Empire</b> - one emperor, delegated lackeys</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>infrastructure</b> too hard to maintain</li><li class=\"d0\">Instead: <b>king of a prominent city</b> - military, trade, natural resources</li><li class=\"d1\">extends influence over neighboring towns, villages</li><li class=\"d1\">more like <b>the mob</b> than statecraft: <b>tribute, taxes</b> - \"insurance money\"</li><li class=\"d1\">keeps the local king in power if <b>a deal</b> is made; recalcitrant → <b>replaced</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Genesis: <b>War of the Five Kings</b></li><li class=\"d1\">northern cities had subjugated <b>Sodom and Gomorrah</b> et al.</li><li class=\"d1\">cities of the plain \"smelled weakness\", <b>stopped paying</b></li><li class=\"d1\">northern kings came, took <b>goods, people as slaves</b>; <b>Lot captured</b>, Abraham rescued</li></ul>"},{"t":1322.73,"lv":3,"h":"Suzerain and vassal","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Higher king = <b>suzerain</b>; lesser rulers = <b>vassals</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>suzerainty treaty</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not negotiated - <b>dictated</b> by the party in power</li></ul>"},{"t":1377.03,"lv":2,"h":"Structure of the treaty","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Fixed structure:</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Who I am</b> and <b>what I've done for you</b> - \"I have brought peace to your lands… isn't that nice of me?\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>responsibilities</b> of lower party - \"in gratitude… you will do…\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>blessings</b> if you comply; <b>curses</b> if you don't</li></ul>"},{"t":1435.3,"lv":3,"h":"Two copies, not two tablets","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Back in <b>Exodus 20</b>: not \"four on one tablet, ran out of room\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>two copies</b> of the whole treaty</li><li class=\"d0\">Human version: one at the feet of the <b>idol of the god</b> called as witness; one to the <b>vassal king</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>divine judge</b> to enforce</li><li class=\"d0\">Here both copies in the <b>Ark of the Covenant</b> = <b>footstool of God's throne</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God is <b>both king and god</b> - the enforcer</li></ul>"},{"t":1508.33,"lv":2,"h":"Deuteronomy follows the treaty format","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Whole book follows it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>ch. 1-4</b>: who God is, what he did - Egypt, wilderness, the land</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>ch. 5 on</b>: commandments - what Israel is responsible for</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>ch. 28-30</b>: <b>blessings and curses</b> - and since God saw it coming, \"<b>when</b> you don't\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Ten Commandments = <b>microcosm</b>, same format</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I am the Lord your God who brought you out of Egypt\" = <b>who he is, what he did</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then commandments; then what will happen</li></ul>"},{"t":1594.62,"lv":3,"h":"Ten Commandments as summary","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Treated as <b>summary of Torah / covenant</b> because of how they're <b>presented</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Rich young man: \"what good thing must I do…?\" → \"what is in the Torah?\" → lists <b>commandments</b>; \"kept from my youth\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Church fathers</b>, all church history</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>little red prayer book</b>: confession prep = Ten Commandments, \"have you done this?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">used as <b>subheadings</b> for life</li><li class=\"d0\">Built into their <b>place in the text</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1704.2,"lv":2,"h":"Kingdom of priests","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not an agreement between <b>a king and peasants</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Called a <b>royal priesthood / kingdom of priests</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Priest part</b>: Israel relates to the nations as priests relate to Israelites</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Feast of Tabernacles</b>: <b>70 bowls</b> offered for the <b>70 nations</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"God loved Israel, hated the rest\" (<b>sorry, Calvinists</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>God saving the world through Israel</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ to the <b>Samaritan woman (St. Photini)</b>: \"salvation is <b>from the Jews</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">always aimed at <b>the whole world</b>; God revealing himself to <b>Egyptians</b>, nations watching</li></ul>"},{"t":1808.97,"lv":2,"h":"Extra ecclesiam nulla salus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Missing piece in <b>extra ecclesiam nulla salus</b> - <b>St. Cyprian</b>: outside the church no salvation</li><li class=\"d1\">causing \"ecclesial anxiety\" among Protestants (<b>if YouTube is to be believed</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">heard as \"everyone not Orthodox goes to hell\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same could be said of <b>Israel</b> in the OT - yet <b>non-Israelites found salvation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not about <b>individual fates</b> in God's judgment</li><li class=\"d1\">about <b>where God is working out salvation</b> in the world</li><li class=\"d1\">NT: still Israel, now <b>the church</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Anyone saved is saved <b>through Christ</b>, through what he's doing in the Orthodox Church, \"even if they're not in it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Test: <b>9th c. BC</b> seeker of Yahweh → go to <b>Jerusalem</b>, become an <b>Israelite</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wouldn't say \"keep hanging out in Moab, worshiping a <b>moon god</b>, it's all good\"</li><li class=\"d1\">today: \"I love Christ, struggling with sins\" → \"<b>come to the Orthodox Church</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2062.18,"lv":3,"h":"The royal part","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Called to be - \"not that they achieve it per se\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ruling function</b>: putting things in order, <b>enacting justice</b> toward the nations</li><li class=\"d1\">nations look at Israel's way of life: \"I gotta find out what that's all about\"</li><li class=\"d1\">treat people \"the way no other people treat other people\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Carries through the church: \"<b>they'll know we're Christians by our love</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not bumper stickers, t-shirts, <b>voting habits</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if we're just like them → <b>not living up to our calling</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2 Peter</b>: make our <b>calling and election sure</b></li><li class=\"d1\">chosen <b>to do</b> something - not done for us</li><li class=\"d1\">teacher, <b>husband and father</b>, business, church office → not \"<b>lords of the Gentiles</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2209.06,"lv":2,"h":"The Ten \"Words\": really nine","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Review from Exodus 20 - \"a year ago\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Two issues</li><li class=\"d1\">originally <b>Ten Words</b>, not Ten Commandments</li><li class=\"d1\">only <b>nine</b> actual commandments in the text, here and Exodus 20</li><li class=\"d0\">Traditions <b>split them differently</b> to get ten</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jewish</b>: \"I am the Lord your God…\" = first <b>word</b> - no commandment but a word</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Protestants and Orthodox</b>: split <b>no other gods</b> / <b>no graven image</b> - grammatically one</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Roman Catholic and Lutheran</b>: split the <b>last</b> - wife vs house, field (<b>5:21</b>)</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem with that split</li><li class=\"d1\">all about <b>coveting</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Exodus has house first</b> → two different ninth commandments</li><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox way \"correct\" - \"honestly, there's only kind of nine\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2447.0,"lv":2,"h":"Deut 5:6-7 - No other gods","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reads <b>5:6</b>: \"I am the Lord your God who brings you out of Egypt, out of the house of bondage\" = <b>who he is, what he's done</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>5:7</b> Orthodox Study Bible: \"no <b>different</b> gods before my <b>face</b>\" - \"not really a coherent sentence\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"face\" = literal Hebrew; usually \"before me\" / <b>presence of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jonah fled from <b>Yahweh's face</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"different\" vs <b>other / alien</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Core: <b>worship no other gods alongside him</b> - only <b>Yahweh, God of Israel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">says nothing on whether those gods <b>exist</b> in some form</li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>break from pagan understanding</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2588.58,"lv":2,"h":"How pagan pantheons worked","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not the \"<b>Justice League</b>\" version: gods on a mountain with superpowers - <b>never</b> how ancient religion worked</li><li class=\"d0\">A city or people group had <b>one or two gods</b></li><li class=\"d1\">lesser spirits: springs, brooks, glades, trees, mountains</li><li class=\"d0\">Larger political units: gods of cities <b>related as siblings</b>, parallel to city relationships</li><li class=\"d1\">top city's god = <b>father</b>; <b>Zeus</b> on top, Athena, Apollo beneath</li><li class=\"d1\">not worshiping a group - seeing gods as <b>related</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A pagan worshiped <b>local gods</b>, plus those of the family's origin, plus those of wherever he <b>moved or traveled</b></li><li class=\"d1\">stop at <b>local temple</b> on a trip</li></ul>"},{"t":2707.14,"lv":3,"h":"Israel's clean break","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">New nation <b>not ethnically united</b></li><li class=\"d1\">descendants of Abraham - <b>Midianites, Jethro, Edomites</b> - worshiped Abraham's God, <b>Yahweh</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>Egyptians</b> and others picked up on the way</li><li class=\"d0\">Egyptian convert: \"keep family's <b>Egyptian gods</b>, add Yahweh\" → <b>No</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not importing anything: \"<b>clean break</b>, you're a new people\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Yahweh, God of Israel</b> = the only God you worship</li></ul>"}]});
